{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 17,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.57,
            0.047,
            0.847,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "A Tale of Three Intersecting Lines"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.31,
            0.086,
            0.695,
            0.211
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_17_image_1.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.171,
            0.229,
            0.828,
            0.267
          ],
          "content": "The blue line is the line with the least rightward bend that doesn’t meet the line $\\bar { \\mathcal { C } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.272,
            0.846,
            0.309
          ],
          "content": "Visually, it is clear that the line that creates the smallest $\\angle \\mathrm { B }$ has to be the one parallel to l. Let us call this parallel line m."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.309,
            0.676,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": "Can you tell the actual value of ∠B be in this case?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.328,
            0.561,
            0.346
          ],
          "content": "[**Hint:** Note that AB is the transversal.]"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.346,
            0.846,
            0.383
          ],
          "content": "We have seen that when two lines are parallel, the internal angles on the same side of the transversal add up to $1 8 0 ^ { \\circ }$ . So $\\angle \\mathrm{B} = 140^{\\circ}.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.383,
            0.846,
            0.42
          ],
          "content": "So, for what values of $\\angle \\mathrm { B } ,$ does a triangle not exist? Does the length AB play any part here?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.419,
            0.848,
            0.477
          ],
          "content": "From the discussion above, it can be seen that the length AB does not play any part in deciding the existence of a triangle. We can say that a triangle does not exist when $\\angle \\mathrm { B }$ is greater than or equal to $1 4 0 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.483,
            0.294,
            0.507
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.509,
            0.848,
            0.567
          ],
          "content": "1. For each of the following angles, find another angle for which a triangle is (a) possible, (b) not possible. Find at least two different angles for each category:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.57,
            0.293,
            0.59
          ],
          "content": "(a) $3 0 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.594,
            0.293,
            0.614
          ],
          "content": "(b) $7 0 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.618,
            0.293,
            0.638
          ],
          "content": "(c) $5 4 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.643,
            0.304,
            0.662
          ],
          "content": "(d) $1 4 4 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.667,
            0.848,
            0.802
          ],
          "content": "2. Determine which of the following pairs can be the angles of a triangle and which cannot: (a) $3 5 ^ { \\circ } , 1 5 0 ^ { \\circ }$ (b) $70^{\\circ},30^{\\circ}$ (c) $90^{\\circ} , 85^{\\circ}$ (d) $5 0 ^ { \\circ } , 1 5 0 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.81,
            0.846,
            0.851
          ],
          "content": "？ Like the triangle inequality, can you form a rule that describes the two angles for which a triangle is possible?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.859,
            0.751,
            0.881
          ],
          "content": "Can the sum of the two angles be used for framing this rule?"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.806,
            0.938,
            0.847,
            0.955
          ],
          "content": ""
        },
